# Served by Cloudflare's static asset handler, not by a Worker (ADR 0013).
#
# No Content-Security-Policy, deliberately, and the omission is worth stating rather than
# leaving to be noticed. Starlight sets the colour theme with an inline script so the page
# does not flash the wrong one, so `script-src 'self'` — the policy apps/web holds to
# (SPEC §57.2) — would break this site, and a policy relaxed to `unsafe-inline` announces a
# protection it does not provide. This site renders no user content and holds no credential,
# which is what makes that trade acceptable here and not on the main site.
/*
  X-Content-Type-Options: nosniff
  Referrer-Policy: strict-origin-when-cross-origin
  X-Frame-Options: DENY

# The generated API description (SPEC §53). Served as JSON so a client can fetch it, and
# cross-origin so a schema explorer running somewhere else can read it — it is a public
# document generated from a public repository.
/openapi.json
  Content-Type: application/json; charset=utf-8
  Access-Control-Allow-Origin: *
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=300, must-revalidate

# The two llms files are markdown, and orator.space serves its own as markdown (§48). These
# are prerendered static files, so the Content-Type the endpoint sets is discarded and the
# asset handler infers text/plain from the .txt extension — which is where the two hostnames
# would otherwise disagree about the same format.
/llms.txt
  Content-Type: text/markdown; charset=utf-8

/llms-full.txt
  Content-Type: text/markdown; charset=utf-8

# The build's own assets, which are content-addressed: Astro puts a hash of the contents in
# every filename under /_astro, and Pagefind does the same for its index shards and fragments.
# A change produces a different name, so these can never be stale and never need revalidating.
#
# Without this they are served with Cloudflare's default for static assets — `max-age=0,
# must-revalidate` — which is right for HTML and wasteful here: every navigation revalidates
# every stylesheet and script, spending a round trip each to be told nothing changed. The main
# site does not have this problem because @astrojs/cloudflare writes the rule itself; there is
# no adapter here, so it is written by hand.
/_astro/*
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=31536000, immutable

/pagefind/fragment/*
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=31536000, immutable

/pagefind/index/*
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=31536000, immutable

# Deliberately not /pagefind/*. pagefind.js and the UI bundles have stable names and their
# contents change from build to build, so an immutable rule there would pin a search index
# loader from a previous deployment in every reader's browser for a year.
